WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.090
EXPLOSIVE GROWTH:
1 million users in 1 year.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.270
This is what a new email client named Skiff
has managed to do.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:12.269
Of course, this is nothing in comparison to
Gmail’s total user count of 1.8 billion

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:13.269
users.

00:00:13.269 --> 00:00:16.119
But, this is also what makes Skiff so impressive.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:20.760
In an era where Gmail is ubiquitous, Skiff
has managed to grow to a million users from

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.860
scratch within a matter of months.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.220
On paper, Skiff doesn’t offer anything remarkable
per se.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.640
They’re just an encrypted email client with
a solid UI.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.770
What’s even more interesting is that Skiff
is able to get away with a much more traditional

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.190
avenue of monetization.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.690
Nowadays, all the craze is to make everything
free and come up with super creative ways

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.950
to monetize users whether that be collecting
data or running ads.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:50.239
I mean this was literally the selling factor
of Gmail: a free invite only email with extraordinary

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:51.239
storage capacity.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:54.969
But, this comes with its own set of disadvantages.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:01:00.199
For one, Google is an ad company who makes
money by getting to know you as well as possible.

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:03.840
And what better way to get to know you than
reading our emails.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.330
This was actually a big controversy back in
the day and Google apparantly stopped reading

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.030
our emails back in 2017.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.740
But, certain smart features suggest the opposite
so you can take that for what you will.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.270
Skiff on the other hand can’t read your
emails even if they wanted to because everything

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.630
is end to end encrypted.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:26.850
As such, they have turned to a more archaic
mode of monetization where people actually

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.540
pay the company directly for the service.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.880
Maybe some of you are too young to know about
this but this is how companies used to do

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.970
business and Skiff is trying to bring this
model back to one of the most competitive

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.970
industries in the world.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:45.399
While they do have a generous free tier with
10 gb of storage and even custom domain support,

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:50.409
the only way they make money is from people
who spring for the Essential or Pro tier.

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:53.750
Given their user count though, it doesn’t
seem like this is really slowing down their

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.750
adoption.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.189
They even have some super notable users like
Vitalik Buterin.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:01.789
But, it’s not all sunshines and rainbows.

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:05.369
To get this point, Skiff has had to raise
over $14 million.

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:10.670
Also, while Skiff does have quite a bit of
momentum right now, it’s not clear if they’ll

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.260
be able to maintain this momentum after they
burn through the privacy and UI centric folks.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.790
The reality is that while the average person
would like to have more privacy, they’re

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.780
also too lazy to change anything they’re
currently doing.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:28.280
So, join me as we take a look at whether Skiff
is truly the next big thing or if it’s just

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:35.260
a niche product that’ll never be more than
just that.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.450
THE CASE FOR SKIFF:
The main appealing factors of Skiff are pretty

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:38.450
simple.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:42.480
It’s probably the best looking mail client
on the market and it has privacy that even

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.849
experts seem to like.

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:48.260
In fact, all of Skiff’s code is open source,
so if you’re a coder, you can take a look

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.510
at exactly what the platform tracks, how it
encrypts your emails, and whether they’re

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.230
actually being honest.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.750
Even if you're not a coder, you can rest assured
that everything truly is private as Russia

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.790
has given it the seal of approval by banning
it.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:07.420
Skiff is also trying address traditional shortcoming
regarding mail filters and centralization

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.260
but really the selling factors are the UI
and privacy.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.849
That’s pretty much it, but oftentimes that’s
all you need.

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:19.310
When you’re addressing something as established
as email, there’s really not much you can

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.900
change but that’s ok.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.989
You don’t need to revolutionize email, you
just have to make email noticeably better.

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:29.340
I mean this has been Google’s secret sauce
the entire time.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.810
They were never first to anything they did.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.879
There were plenty of search engines before
Google, plenty of emails before Gmail, and

00:03:35.879 --> 00:03:37.950
plenty of browsers before Chrome.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.489
Fun fact, Google’s own CEO, Eric Schmidt,
was strongly opposed to building Chrome as

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:45.310
he felt that entering the browser wars was
futile.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.221
In fact, he single handedly delayed the launch
of Chrome by 6 years but that didn’t stop

00:03:50.221 --> 00:03:54.150
Chrome from being the biggest success the
browser space has ever seen.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:59.739
The truth is that while first movers do indeed
have an advantage, that’s all it is: an

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:00.739
advantage.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:03.220
It’s not endless monopoly over a certain
domain.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:08.110
I mean, ChatGPT is disrupting Google as we
speak which brings us into the our next point

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.670
for Skiff which is the sentiment surrounding
Google.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.540
One of the biggest reasons that Google was
so successful was because of their underdog

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.540
nature.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.519
People loved to support the scrappy startup
that was Google.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:22.910
Their motto of “Don’t be evil” resonated
with users.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.610
Why support hotmail and internet explorer,
products from the evil tech monopoly, Microsoft,

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.480
when you could support Google.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:34.020
But while this worked in Google’s favor
back in the 2000s, this is no longer the case.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.970
Today, Google is very much the evil tech monopoly
that Microsoft was back in the day meaning

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.289
that Google is primed for disruption.

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:44.850
I mean just take a look at how quick people
were to laugh at Bard.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:50.100
Sure, Bard made a mistake but if Google was
still a scrappy startup, I bet people would’ve

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.550
focused on the millions of items that Bard
did correctly as opposed to the few that Bard

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:55.550
didn’t.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:05:00.199
But since Google is a giant the sentiment
is: oh Google is a trillion dollar monopoly

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.840
and can’t even get this right?

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.090
What a joke?

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.430
This shift in sentiment is subtle but it makes
all the difference.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.570
All of a sudden, a service like Skiff that
actually respects your privacy is worth a

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:11.570
shot.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.260
What’s the worst that could happen?

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.139
It sucks and you come back to Gmail?

00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:17.520
Clearly not very high stakes.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.840
Speaking of high stakes, Skiff doesn’t have
a very high barrier for entry.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.910
One of the advantages of entering an established
market is that people are already familiar

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:27.240
with the product.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.150
People already know why email is useful, how
to send an email, what cc and bcc mean.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:37.000
So, Skiff doesn’t have to convince people
on adopting email, they just have to convince

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.260
people that Skiff is the best platform for
email.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.250
And given that most people are relatively
tech savvy nowadays, getting them to give

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.550
it a shot isn’t too hard.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:49.480
This is why we see so many apps blow up basically
overnight.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:54.259
In fact, if you truly have a winning product,
it’s easier to go viral today than ever

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:55.259
before.

00:05:55.259 --> 00:05:58.430
TikTok gained 100 million users within a matter
of 9 months.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:02.820
More recently, ChatGPT was able to garner
100 million users within just 2 months.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:07.880
20 years ago, such traction would’ve been
impossible regardless how good the product

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:08.880
was.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.030
Nowadays, information is simply shared and
absorbed so quickly that if you have momentum

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.510
on your side, you can go extremely far.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:20.520
So, trying to challenge even something as
big as Gmail is very much possible but there’s

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:24.200
of course some pretty big hurdles along the
way as well.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:29.780
THE CASE AGAINST SKIFF:
Likely the biggest elephant in the room is

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.780
who cares?

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:35.729
Nowadays, we all see articles about Facebook
and Google and basically every other company

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:37.070
invading our privacy.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:42.370
And while I do believe that everyone should
have the right to privacy, realistically speaking,

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:45.910
I think the vast majority of us have become
numb to the idea.

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:50.230
I mean, is having a truly private email really
going to change anything?

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:54.259
Are you also going to find alternatives for
Instagram and YouTube and Google Search and

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:56.639
TikTok and basically everything else as well?

00:06:56.639 --> 00:06:59.160
I’m gonna guess probably not.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.900
This isn’t to say that these companies are
in the right for collecting data on all of

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:03.900
us.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.970
But I guess it’s simply a reality that most
of us have just come to accept as reality.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:12.500
So, it’s not clear if the privacy argument
will really hit as well as you might think.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.180
Speaking of privacy, it’s also not clear
how much people actually care about privacy

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.710
when it comes to email in general.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:22.169
I don’t know about you but virtually all
of my email use is professional.

00:07:22.169 --> 00:07:25.490
The last time I actually emailed a friend
was like 2011.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:29.760
So, there’s not anything espeically personal
that I send through email that I would want

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.470
to protect to begin with.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:39.190
Most people use email for work or school to
contact coworkers, managers, professors, and

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:40.190
so on.

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:44.041
Or they use it to sign up for newsletters
and create accounts but very rarely do people

00:07:44.041 --> 00:07:46.140
use email for social communication.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:50.470
So again, not sure how much people will care
about privacy here.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.990
Something else to note is that people don’t
really choose what email client they use for

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:57.400
work or school unless its like the Apple mail
app.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.050
They just use whatever the school or company
uses.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:04.120
Skiff’s new feature that allows people to
use a custom domain for free does technically

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.200
make this possible but we’ll have to see
how many people actually care to take advantage

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.200
of this feature.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.150
Also, there’s no way we can discuss the
future of Skiff without mentioning ProtonMail.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:18.230
If you’re not familiar with ProtonMail it’s
basically just an older Skiff.

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:22.870
ProtonMail launched back in 2014 with the
promise of better privacy and a more refined

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.139
UI and they’re doing pretty good.

00:08:25.139 --> 00:08:30.919
They have over 70 million accounts but this
brings up the question: what does Skiff really

00:08:30.919 --> 00:08:32.680
offer over ProtonMail.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:37.640
Well, we can’t really make an argument based
on UI because that’s subjective.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.500
And to be honest, they both have a great modern
UI so it’s really just personal preference.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.710
There’s also of course some minor differences
in terms of pricing and features but really

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.310
the main difference seems to be the encryption
standard.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:54.080
ProtonMail uses something called PGP encryption
which has been the standard for decades.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.050
Skiff argues that PGP encryption is outdated
and that there are far better encryption standards

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:01.170
out there and they could be absolutely right.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.590
But how many people actually care about this?

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:10.140
As far as the average user is concerned, both
email clients are end to end encrypted.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:14.170
Also, all email clients will claim that they
have the best encryption.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.490
So unless you really understand these encryption
standards, it doesn’t really mean anything

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.070
to the average person.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:25.530
And all of that brings us into likely Skiff’s
biggest shortall which is monetization.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:29.890
Every dominant email that has ever existed
has come from a tech giant whether that be

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:33.090
Aol, Yahoo, Microsoft, or Google.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:37.730
And the main reason for that is that email
simply works much better as a side business

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.290
than a main business.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:43.540
You simply have to reach extraordinary scale
to start make any sort of profit.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:48.250
ProtonMail for example is only possible because
of the generosity of its community.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:52.050
And it seems that the only reason that Skiff
and their generous free tiers are possible

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.930
are because of insane VC money.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:59.390
VC are willing to go quite far nowadays in
terms of funding but it’s not the same Google

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:02.589
launching Gmail in 2004 and subsidizing the
cost themselves.

00:10:02.589 --> 00:10:08.079
As far as Google is concerned, even if Gmail
is a breakeven effort indefinitely or even

00:10:08.079 --> 00:10:13.970
a slight loss, that’s a massive win as 1.8
billion users are even more integrated into

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:15.269
the Google ecosystem.

00:10:15.269 --> 00:10:19.640
For Skiff, on the other hand, monetization
has to eventually work out and they have to

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.240
fundraise in the meantime.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.620
Otherwise, their toast.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:28.800
THE FUTURE OF SKIFF:
While launching a new email client in the

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:33.050
2020s may seem crazy, it’s actually way
more viable than you think.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:37.180
The sentiment against Google paired with the
insane virality potential of modern apps makes

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:39.210
a new email client quite viable.

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:44.230
Combine this with some extra privacy and a
better UI and Skiff may very well have a winning

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:48.649
product on their hands but their success is
by no means guaranteed.

00:10:48.649 --> 00:10:52.990
Several companies have picked up on this trend
of adding privacy and UI to replace offerings

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.820
from big tech companies.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.380
Some other examples are Signal, BeReal, and
ProtonMail.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:02.860
Some of theses anti big tech offerings end
up panning out while others end up dead in

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:04.010
the water.

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:08.620
Only time will tell if Skiff has enough mojo
to convince the general public to make the

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:09.620
switch.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:13.470
Schools are already ditching Google in general
so that may be an opportunity for Google.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:20.489
Check out this video to learn more about why
schools are ditching Google.

